<div class="siem-content blue-debrief" id="blue-debrief" style="display:none;flex:1;overflow-y:auto;">
  <div class="blue-debrief-label">Debrief — Phishing Campaign Analysis</div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Campaign Summary</h3>
    <p>The attacker spoofed an email from <code>security@meridian-corp.com</code> using the sending infrastructure at <code>mail.evil-phish.ru</code> (IP 91.234.56.78). The email impersonated the IT Security Team and used urgency ("password expiration in 24 hours") to pressure users into clicking a malicious link.</p>
    <p>The payload URL was Base64-encoded to evade email gateway link scanners: <code>aHR0cHM6Ly9tZXJpZGlhbi1jb3JwLWxvZ2luLmV2aWwtcGhpc2gucnUvYXV0aC9sb2dpbg==</code> decoded to <code>https://meridian-corp-login.evil-phish.ru/auth/login</code> — a credential harvesting page that mimicked the company's SSO portal.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Why the gateway missed it</h3>
    <p><strong>SPF failed</strong> but the gateway was set to "soft fail" mode (accept and tag). The email was delivered with a <code>[EXTERNAL]</code> tag that many users ignore.</p>
    <p><strong>No DKIM signature</strong> — the gateway logged the missing signature but didn't block the email because DKIM-unsigned emails are common from legitimate senders with poor email hygiene.</p>
    <p><strong>DMARC failed</strong> but the domain's DMARC policy was set to <code>p=none</code> (monitor only). Had it been <code>p=reject</code>, the email would have been blocked.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Email authentication explained</h3>
    <p><strong>SPF (Sender Policy Framework):</strong> A DNS TXT record listing which mail servers are authorized to send email for a domain. The <code>Received-SPF: fail</code> header tells us the sending server (91.234.56.78) is not in meridian-corp.com's SPF record.</p>
    <p><strong>DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail):</strong> A cryptographic signature added by the sending server. Since the email came from evil-phish.ru (not meridian-corp.com), there's no valid DKIM signature for the From: domain.</p>
    <p><strong>DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication):</strong> A policy that tells receiving servers what to do when SPF and DKIM both fail. With <code>p=none</code>, the receiving server only reports — it doesn't block. Changing to <code>p=reject</code> would prevent this attack.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Remediation</h3>
    <p><strong>Immediate:</strong> Force password reset for all 4 users who clicked the link. Revoke their active sessions. Block the domain <code>evil-phish.ru</code> and IP <code>91.234.56.78</code> at the firewall and email gateway.</p>
    <p><strong>Short-term:</strong> Update DMARC policy from <code>p=none</code> to <code>p=quarantine</code> (then <code>p=reject</code> after monitoring). Configure the email gateway to block emails failing both SPF and DKIM.</p>
    <p><strong>Long-term:</strong> Deploy phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2/WebAuthn). Implement URL rewriting and time-of-click analysis. Run regular phishing simulations to train employees.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>In the real world</h3>
    <p>Phishing accounts for <strong>91% of cyberattacks</strong> (Verizon DBIR). Business Email Compromise (BEC) cost organizations $2.7 billion in 2022 alone. The most effective defense is layered: email authentication (SPF/DKIM/DMARC at <code>p=reject</code>), link scanning, user awareness training, and phishing-resistant MFA.</p>
    <p>The 2020 Twitter hack began with a phone-based phishing attack targeting employees. The 2016 DNC breach started with a spear-phishing email containing a short URL. These campaigns typically take minutes to execute but can take months to fully remediate.</p>
  </div>
</div>

